{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc | sed -n '45,210p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '1320,1510p'\"","aggregated_output":"    45\t(defn require-call-pair!\n    46\t  \"Fail when the VM holds no FFI pair. Callers must run this before parking:\n    47\t   an error raised after `park-continuation` strands a continuation in\n    48\t   `:parked` and consumes an id counter.\"\n    49\t  [resources what]\n    50\t  (or (call-pair resources)\n    51\t      (throw (ex-info \"This VM was constructed without an FFI call pair, so it cannot make a dao.stream.apply call\"\n    52\t                      {:what what}))))\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\t(defn call-id\n    56\t  \"The id of the FFI call a VM is about to park, given the local park id\n    57\t   `yin.vm.engine/park-id` answers for it: `[caller-token local-id]` when\n    58\t   the VM was built with `:ffi-caller-id`, else `local-id` itself. The\n    59\t   call parks under this id and its request carries it, so a response on\n    60\t   a call-out shared by several callers correlates to exactly one call.\"\n    61\t  [state local-id]\n    62\t  (if-some [token (:ffi-caller-id state)]\n    63\t    [token local-id]\n    64\t    local-id))\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(defn response-call-id\n    68\t  \"The call id a parked FFI response reader waits for, or nil when\n    69\t   `entry` is no such reader. Both VM shapes: semantic, stack and\n    70\t   register place `:call-id` on the entry; the walker nests it in its\n    71\t   `:dao.stream.apply/eval-call` continuation.\"\n    72\t  [entry]\n    73\t  (when (= :next (:reason entry))\n    74\t    (let [k (:k entry)]\n    75\t      (or (:call-id entry)\n    76\t          (when (and (map? k) (= :dao.stream.apply/eval-call (:type k)))\n    77\t            (:call-id k))))))\n    78\t\n    79\t\n    80\t(defn request-call-id\n    81\t  \"The call id of a retained FFI request writer, or nil: the entry that,\n    82\t   once its append lands and it is restored, becomes the reader for that\n    83\t   id. Both VM shapes, as `response-call-id`.\"\n    84\t  [entry]\n    85\t  (let [k (:k entry)]\n    86\t    (cond (:request-sent entry) (:call-id entry)\n    87\t          (and (map? k) (= :dao.stream.apply/request-sent (:type k)))\n    88\t          (:parked-id k))))\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\t(def response-loss-statuses\n    92\t  \"The wake statuses of an FFI response reader whose response stream\n    93\t   can no longer answer it: it ended, or it lost values to a gap.\"\n    94\t  #{::response-ended ::response-gap})\n    95\t\n    96\t\n    97\t(defn response-lost\n    98\t  \"The portable apply error for a call whose response stream ended\n    99\t   (`::response-ended`) or skipped over evicted values (`::response-gap`)\n   100\t   before this call was answered. Both carry the terminal code\n   101\t   `:dao.stream.apply/ended`; `::loss` tells them apart. The caller\n   102\t   reports only the loss it observed -- it cannot know why the responder\n   103\t   closed the stream or what the gap held.\"\n   104\t  [call-id status]\n   105\t  (let [gap? (= ::response-gap status)]\n   106\t    {:call-id call-id,\n   107\t     :error {:dao.stream.apply/code :dao.stream.apply/ended,\n   108\t             :dao.stream.apply/message\n   109\t             (if gap?\n   110\t               \"FFI response stream lost values to a gap before this call was answered\"\n   111\t               \"FFI response stream ended before this call was answered\"),\n   112\t             ::loss (if gap? :dao.stream/gap :dao.stream/end)}}))\n   113\t\n   114\t\n   115\t(defn throw-response-lost!\n   116\t  \"Raise `response-lost` as `call-result` raises an apply error.\"\n   117\t  [call-id status]\n   118\t  (let [data (response-lost call-id status)]\n   119\t    (throw (ex-info (str \"FFI call failed: \"\n   120\t                         (get-in data [:error :dao.stream.apply/message]))\n   121\t                    data))))\n   122\t\n   123\t\n   124\t(defn call-response-wait-entry\n   125\t  \"The polling wait entry for a sent call awaiting its correlated response.\"\n   126\t  [call-id next-k env]\n   127\t  {:k {:type :dao.stream.apply/eval-call,\n   128\t       :next next-k,\n   129\t       :env env,\n   130\t       :call-id call-id},\n   131\t   :env env,\n   132\t   :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key},\n   133\t   :reason :next,\n   134\t   :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key})\n   135\t\n   136\t\n   137\t(defn response-wait-entry\n   138\t  \"The polling wait entry for a sent call awaiting its correlated response,\n   139\t   for an arbitrary register payload (`yin.vm.engine.md` section 7, edit 3).\n   140\t\n   141\t   `entry` is a wait, ready, or parked record as the VM built it: the\n   142\t   writer entry that retried the request, or the registers of a call whose\n   143\t   request was sent at once. The FFI keys of the writer step\n   144\t   (`:request-sent`, `:op`) and its retried `:datom` are removed; the\n   145\t   call-out reader keys are added. Every other key -- the VM's register\n   146\t   payload, whatever its shape -- is preserved verbatim, so no VM has to\n   147\t   know this layout. `call-response-wait-entry` (the walker's frame-typed\n   148\t   shape) is unchanged.\"\n   149\t  [entry call-id]\n   150\t  (-> entry\n   151\t      (dissoc :value :status :cursor :resource-updates :stream\n   152\t              :datom :type :id :request-sent :op)\n   153\t      (assoc :call-id call-id\n   154\t             :reason :next\n   155\t             :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key}\n   156\t             :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key)))\n   157\t\n   158\t\n   159\t(defn call-result\n   160\t  \"Unwrap a response envelope for the continuation that made the call.\n   161\t\n   162\t   v1 read `:dao.stream.apply/value` off a response that could only succeed.\n   163\t   A v2 response carries exactly one of `ok` or `error`, and correlation is\n   164\t   checked here rather than assumed from stream order.\"\n   165\t  [response call-id]\n   166\t  (when-not (apply2/response? response)\n   167\t    (throw (ex-info \"FFI response envelope is malformed\" {:response response})))\n   168\t  (when (and call-id (not= call-id (apply2/response-id response)))\n   169\t    (throw (ex-info \"FFI response does not correlate with this parked call\"\n   170\t                    {:call-id call-id,\n   171\t                     :response-id (apply2/response-id response)})))\n   172\t  (if-let [err (apply2/response-error response)]\n   173\t    (throw (ex-info (str \"FFI call failed: \"\n   174\t                         (:dao.stream.apply/message err))\n   175\t                    {:call-id call-id, :error err}))\n   176\t    (apply2/response-ok response)))\n   177\t\n   178\t\n   179\t(defn normalize\n   180\t  \"Normalize bridge input to explicit bridge state, without minting.\n   181\t\n   182\t   Accepted forms: nil, a handler map, or an explicit\n   183\t   {:handlers ... :cursor ...} map.\"\n   184\t  [bridge]\n   185\t  (cond (nil? bridge) nil\n   186\t        (and (map? bridge) (contains? bridge :handlers))\n   187\t        (update bridge :handlers #(or % {}))\n   188\t        (map? bridge) {:handlers bridge}\n   189\t        :else (throw (ex-info\n   190\t                       \"Bridge must be nil, a handler map, or {:handlers ... :cursor ...}\"\n   191\t                       {:bridge bridge}))))\n   192\t\n   193\t\n   194\t(defn attach\n   195\t  \"Attach explicit bridge state to a built VM, minting the bridge cursor on\n   196\t   the call-in stream when the bridge does not carry one.\"\n   197\t  [vm bridge]\n   198\t  (if-let [bridge* (normalize bridge)]\n   199\t    (let [{:keys [call-in]} (require-call-pair! (:resources vm) :bridge)\n   200\t          cursor (or (:cursor bridge*) (vm/mint-oldest call-in :bridge))]\n   201\t      (assoc vm :bridge (assoc bridge* :cursor cursor)))\n   202\t    vm))\n   203\t\n   204\t\n   205\t(defn bridge-from-opts\n   206\t  \"Read bridge configuration from create-vm opts. `:bridge` is the canonical\n   207\t   key. Minting happens in `attach`, which runs after the pair exists.\"\n   208\t  [opts]\n   209\t  (normalize (:bridge opts)))\n   210\t\n  1320\t;; woken reader takes whatever value its cell holds next -- and\n  1321\t;; `yin.vm.ffi/call-result` could only check the id after the value was\n  1322\t;; consumed. The router matches before consuming: per response cell it\n  1323\t;; reads a bounded run of values, advances the cell on every `ok`, and\n  1324\t;; wakes only the waiter whose call id the response names. A response\n  1325\t;; for no live waiter is discarded with a diagnostic; `call-result`\n  1326\t;; stays the final validation of the matched envelope.\n  1327\t\n  1328\t(def ffi-response-budget\n  1329\t  \"How many values the router reads from one response cell per wait-set\n  1330\t   check. A run of other callers' responses longer than this is read\n  1331\t   across later checks.\"\n  1332\t  64)\n  1333\t\n  1334\t\n  1335\t(def ^:private ffi-diagnostics-cap\n  1336\t  \"How many unmatched-response diagnostics a VM keeps. A shared call-out\n  1337\t   carries every caller's responses, so skipping others' is ordinary\n  1338\t   traffic: the record is bounded, oldest dropped.\"\n  1339\t  64)\n  1340\t\n  1341\t\n  1342\t(defn- ffi-response-entry?\n  1343\t  \"True for a parked FFI response reader the router polls.\"\n  1344\t  [entry]\n  1345\t  (and (some? (ffi/response-call-id entry))\n  1346\t       (map? (:cursor-ref entry))))\n  1347\t\n  1348\t\n  1349\t(defn- ffi-skip\n  1350\t  \"Record a response read off `cell` that no live waiter owns.\"\n  1351\t  [state cell v]\n  1352\t  (let [id (apply2/response-id v)\n  1353\t        d (if (some? id)\n  1354\t            {:kind :unmatched, :response-id id, :cell cell}\n  1355\t            {:kind :malformed, :cell cell})]\n  1356\t    (-> state\n  1357\t        (update :ffi-diagnostics\n  1358\t                (fn [ds]\n  1359\t                  (let [ds (conj (vec ds) d)]\n  1360\t                    (if (> (count ds) ffi-diagnostics-cap)\n  1361\t                      (subvec ds (- (count ds) ffi-diagnostics-cap))\n  1362\t                      ds))))\n  1363\t        (telemetry/emit-snapshot :ffi-skip d))))\n  1364\t\n  1365\t\n  1366\t(defn take-ffi-diagnostics\n  1367\t  \"Return `[diagnostics state']`: the responses the router discarded\n  1368\t   since the last take, oldest first, and the state without them.\"\n  1369\t  [state]\n  1370\t  [(vec (:ffi-diagnostics state)) (dissoc state :ffi-diagnostics)])\n  1371\t\n  1372\t\n  1373\t(defn- read-response\n  1374\t  \"One read of a response cell, folded as `dao.stream.waitset` folds its\n  1375\t   own reads: an answer the contract cannot vouch for, or a throw, is\n  1376\t   `:dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer`.\"\n  1377\t  [handle cursor]\n  1378\t  (try\n  1379\t    (let [r (stream/next handle cursor)\n  1380\t          defect (stream/validate-outcome :next r)]\n  1381\t      (if (and defect (not= :unauthorized-outcome (:error defect)))\n  1382\t        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer}\n  1383\t        r))\n  1384\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n  1385\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer})))\n  1386\t\n  1387\t\n  1388\t(defn- poll-ffi-cell\n  1389\t  \"Route one response cell: read at most `budget` values from its\n  1390\t   cursor, waking each waiter in `entries` whose call id a response\n  1391\t   names. A response whose id belongs to a woken request writer that has\n  1392\t   not yet become a reader (`pending`) stops the run without being\n  1393\t   consumed, so it is read again once that reader waits. `end` wakes the\n  1394\t   remaining waiters as `::ffi/response-ended`, `gap` as\n  1395\t   `::ffi/response-gap`; any other outcome wakes them under its own\n  1396\t   keyword, as the generic sweep would. Returns `[state waiting woken]`,\n  1397\t   woken as `dao.stream.waitset` results.\"\n  1398\t  [state cell-key entries pending budget]\n  1399\t  (let [cell (get (:resources state) cell-key)\n  1400\t        handle (when (map? cell) (get (:resources state) (:stream-id cell)))]\n  1401\t    (if (nil? handle)\n  1402\t      [state []\n  1403\t       (mapv (fn [e] {:entry e, :status :dao.stream.waitset/unresolved})\n  1404\t             entries)]\n  1405\t      (loop [state state\n  1406\t             cursor (:cursor cell)\n  1407\t             waiting entries\n  1408\t             woken []\n  1409\t             n 0]\n  1410\t        (let [done (fn [state cursor waiting woken]\n  1411\t                     [(assoc-in state [:resources cell-key :cursor] cursor)\n  1412\t                      waiting woken])\n  1413\t              wake-all (fn [status value]\n  1414\t                         (into woken\n  1415\t                               (map (fn [e]\n  1416\t                                      {:entry e,\n  1417\t                                       :status status,\n  1418\t                                       :value value}))\n  1419\t                               waiting))]\n  1420\t          (if (or (empty? waiting) (>= n budget))\n  1421\t            (done state cursor waiting woken)\n  1422\t            (let [r (read-response handle cursor)\n  1423\t                  o (outcome r)]\n  1424\t              (case o\n  1425\t                :dao.stream/ok\n  1426\t                (let [v (:dao.stream/value r)\n  1427\t                      id (apply2/response-id v)\n  1428\t                      owner (when (some? id)\n  1429\t                              (first (filter #(= id (ffi/response-call-id %))\n  1430\t                                             waiting)))]\n  1431\t                  (cond\n  1432\t                    owner (recur state\n  1433\t                                 (:dao.stream/cursor r)\n  1434\t                                 (filterv #(not (identical? owner %)) waiting)\n  1435\t                                 (conj woken\n  1436\t                                       {:entry owner, :status :ok, :value v})\n  1437\t                                 (inc n))\n  1438\t                    (and (some? id) (contains? pending id))\n  1439\t                    (done state cursor waiting woken)\n  1440\t                    :else (recur (ffi-skip state cell-key v)\n  1441\t                                 (:dao.stream/cursor r)\n  1442\t                                 waiting\n  1443\t                                 woken\n  1444\t                                 (inc n))))\n  1445\t                :dao.stream/blocked (done state cursor waiting woken)\n  1446\t                :dao.stream/end\n  1447\t                (done state cursor [] (wake-all ::ffi/response-ended nil))\n  1448\t                :dao.stream/gap\n  1449\t                (done state\n  1450\t                      (:dao.stream/cursor r)\n  1451\t                      []\n  1452\t                      (wake-all ::ffi/response-gap :dao.stream/gap))\n  1453\t                (done state cursor [] (wake-all o o))))))))))\n  1454\t\n  1455\t\n  1456\t(defn- poll-ffi-responses\n  1457\t  \"Route every parked FFI response reader, per response cell in wait-set\n  1458\t   order -- the call-out cursor and the carried cells of lowered\n  1459\t   retained calls alike. Returns the state with the still-waiting readers\n  1460\t   appended to `others` and the woken ones queued.\"\n  1461\t  [state entries others]\n  1462\t  (if (empty? entries)\n  1463\t    state\n  1464\t    (let [pending (into #{}\n  1465\t                        (keep ffi/request-call-id)\n  1466\t                        (:ready-queue state))\n  1467\t          cells (distinct (map #(:id (:cursor-ref %)) entries))\n  1468\t          [state waiting woken]\n  1469\t          (reduce (fn [[state waiting woken] cell-key]\n  1470\t                    (let [[state w k] (poll-ffi-cell\n  1471\t                                        state\n  1472\t                                        cell-key\n  1473\t                                        (filterv #(= cell-key\n  1474\t                                                     (:id (:cursor-ref %)))\n  1475\t                                                 entries)\n  1476\t                                        pending\n  1477\t                                        ffi-response-budget)]\n  1478\t                      [state (into waiting w) (into woken k)]))\n  1479\t                  [state [] []]\n  1480\t                  cells)]\n  1481\t      (-> state\n  1482\t          (assoc :wait-set (into (vec others) waiting))\n  1483\t          (update :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n  1484\t                  (make-woken-run-queue-entries state woken))))))\n  1485\t\n  1486\t\n  1487\t(defn check-wait-set\n  1488\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n  1489\t\n  1490\t   The sweep is `dao.stream.waitset/check` — this engine was that library's\n  1491\t   seed and is now its first consumer. Every entry is resolved to a live\n  1492\t   handle and an opaque cursor out of the store by `waitset-resolver` and\n  1493\t   polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution happens per\n  1494\t   poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n  1495\t   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n  1496\t\n  1497\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n  1498\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n  1499\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values —\n  1500\t   the value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next —\n  1501\t   instead of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll\n  1502\t   cursor. An entry that stays waiting is retained in its stored,\n  1503\t   resource-id form.\n  1504\t\n  1505\t   Each woken result's `:status` is stamped onto its ready entry, where\n  1506\t   `terminal-resume-outcome` reads it: an outcome the immediate operation\n  1507\t   raises as an error fails the same way when the resume pops it — and a\n  1508\t   waitset diagnostic, an entry the sweep could not poll at all, raises\n  1509\t   there too, before any continuation is restored.\n  1510\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
